-- ---- s 58 charged interests ----------------------------------------------

GIVETH A `Charged interest`
`the mortgaged flat` MEANS
    `Charged interest` WITH `description` IS "a Housing and Development Board flat subject to a legal mortgage"
                          , `value` IS 500000
                          , `charged with the payment of money at the time of his death` IS TRUE
                          , `a contrary or other intention signified by will, deed or other document` IS FALSE
                          , `that intention signified only by a general direction for the payment of debts or by a charge of debts upon the residuary estate` IS FALSE

-- The will contains the common boilerplate "I direct that my debts be paid
-- out of my residuary estate". s 58(3)(a) says that is NOT a contrary
-- intention, so the flat still carries its own mortgage.
GIVETH A `Charged interest`
`the mortgaged flat with a general direction to pay debts` MEANS
    `Charged interest` WITH `description` IS "the same flat, under a will directing that debts be paid out of the residuary estate"
                          , `value` IS 500000
                          , `charged with the payment of money at the time of his death` IS TRUE
                          , `a contrary or other intention signified by will, deed or other document` IS TRUE
                          , `that intention signified only by a general direction for the payment of debts or by a charge of debts upon the residuary estate` IS TRUE

-- The will says, in terms, that the mortgage on the flat is to be discharged
-- out of the residue. Words "expressly ... referring to all or some part of
-- the charge", so s 58(1) yields.
GIVETH A `Charged interest`
`the mortgaged flat with an express direction about the mortgage` MEANS
    `Charged interest` WITH `description` IS "the same flat, under a will directing that the mortgage on it be discharged out of the residuary estate"
                          , `value` IS 500000
                          , `charged with the payment of money at the time of his death` IS TRUE
                          , `a contrary or other intention signified by will, deed or other document` IS TRUE
                          , `that intention signified only by a general direction for the payment of debts or by a charge of debts upon the residuary estate` IS FALSE


§§ `Assertions`

-- Each assertion names the provision it tests. They are grouped in the Act's
-- order, not in order of importance, so that a reviewer working down the
-- statute meets each one where it belongs.

§§§ `Which grant — ss 8, 13, 18, 20, 62`

-- s 8(1): a will, and an executor who survives, has not renounced, is not an
-- infant and is not legally incapable. Probate.
#ASSERT `the name of the grant in` `the Alice probate` EQUALS "probate"

-- s 13(1)(b): the sole executor has renounced. Letters of administration with
-- the will annexed -- the will still governs the distribution, but the court
-- chooses the administrator.
#ASSERT `the name of the grant in` `the Alice estate on failure of executors` EQUALS "letters of administration with the will annexed"

-- s 18(1): no will. Letters of administration.
#ASSERT `the name of the grant in` `the Bernard intestacy` EQUALS "letters of administration"

-- s 20: a probate action is pending, so the grant is the limited one and the
-- administrator may not distribute -- even though the will and its executor
-- are exactly as they were in the probate case above.
#ASSERT `the name of the grant in` `the contested Alice estate` EQUALS "letters of administration pending probate action"

-- s 62(1): $40,000 in Singapore, no application pending, and the Public
-- Trustee has declared. No grant of representation at all.
#ASSERT `the Public Trustee may undertake to administer the property in` `the small estate administered by the Public Trustee`
#ASSERT `the name of the grant in` `the small estate administered by the Public Trustee` EQUALS "no grant of representation is required"

-- s 13(1) against the shared ontology's coarse field. The Will record carries
-- `an executor is appointed, and able and willing to act`, which is the same
-- question s 13(1) asks in five limbs; the two must not drift apart.
#ASSERT NOT `there is a failure of executors in` `the Alice probate`
#ASSERT `there is a failure of executors in` `the Alice estate on failure of executors`

-- s 13(2): a universal or residuary legatee (rank 1) has the prior right over
-- a creditor (rank 5). One person, not two.
#ASSERT count (`those with a prior right under section 13(2) in` `the Alice estate on failure of executors`) EQUALS 1

-- s 18(4): the widow applies, so s 18(4)(c) -- "when no such person applies"
-- -- never reaches the creditor. Both Grace and Farid have asked, so this
-- fixture alone cannot tell a correct encoding of limb (c) from one that
-- never reads the applications at all; the name of the person it points at is
-- asserted, and the group below supplies the facts that discriminate.
#ASSERT count (`those who may be granted letters of administration under section 18(4) in` `the Bernard intestacy`) EQUALS 1
#ASSERT map (GIVEN p YIELD p's `name`) (`those who may be granted letters of administration under section 18(4) in` `the Bernard intestacy`) EQUALS LIST "Grace"

-- s 18(4)(c): "when no such person applies, letters of administration may be
-- granted to a creditor of the deceased". The widow is alive and is next of
-- kin, but she has not asked; the creditor has. The creditor takes it -- and
-- the widow, who is not applying, is not a person to whom this grant would be
-- made.
#ASSERT map (GIVEN p YIELD p's `name`) (`those who may be granted letters of administration under section 18(4) in` `the Bernard intestacy where only the creditor applies`) EQUALS LIST "Farid"
#ASSERT map (GIVEN p YIELD p's `name`) (`those who may take the grant in` `the Bernard intestacy where only the creditor applies`) EQUALS LIST "Farid"
#ASSERT NOT `a person in the section 18(4)(a) class has applied in` `the Bernard intestacy where only the creditor applies`
#ASSERT `a person in the section 18(4)(a) class has applied in` `the Bernard intestacy`

§§§ `Who may take it — ss 6, 21`

-- s 6(1): "Probate or letters of administration shall not be granted to more
-- than 4 persons in respect of the same property." Five applicants fail.
#ASSERT NOT `the grant complies with section 6(1) in` `the estate with five applicants`
#ASSERT `the grant complies with section 6(1) in` `the Bernard intestacy`
-- But the limit counts the persons a grant is GRANTED to, not the persons who
-- asked. Five have asked and one of them is an infant whom s 21(1) refuses,
-- so the court may grant to four and s 6(1) is not offended.
#ASSERT count (`the applicants for a grant in` `the estate with five applicants one of whom is an infant`) EQUALS 4
#ASSERT `the grant complies with section 6(1) in` `the estate with five applicants one of whom is an infant`

-- s 6(2): with a minority, one individual administrator is not enough.
#ASSERT NOT `the grant complies with section 6(2) in` `the minority with one administrator`
-- Two individuals will do.
#ASSERT `the grant complies with section 6(2) in` `the minority with two administrators`
-- So will a single trust corporation, "with or without an individual".
#ASSERT `the grant complies with section 6(2) in` `the minority with a trust company`
-- Without a minority or life interest the subsection does not bite at all,
-- and a sole administrator is unobjectionable.
#ASSERT `the grant complies with section 6(2) in` `the Family Court administration`
-- s 6(2) constrains "LETTERS of administration", and does not reach probate:
-- a sole executor takes probate lawfully even where a life interest arises
-- under the will, and the Act's answer to the single representative is s 6(4)
-- -- the court's discretionary power to appoint an additional one -- not a
-- bar on the grant.
#ASSERT `the name of the grant in` `the Alice probate where a life interest arises` EQUALS "probate"
#ASSERT `the grant complies with section 6(2) in` `the Alice probate where a life interest arises`
-- It does reach letters of administration WITH THE WILL ANNEXED, which is
-- what "if a life interest arises under the will" contemplates: the same
-- death, the same life interest, one person left who may take the grant, and
-- the subsection is not satisfied.
#ASSERT `the name of the grant in` `the Alice estate on failure of executors, with a life interest` EQUALS "letters of administration with the will annexed"
#ASSERT NOT `the grant complies with section 6(2) in` `the Alice estate on failure of executors, with a life interest`

-- s 21(1): "No probate or letters of administration shall be granted to a
-- person while he is an infant." He is refused even though he is a validly
-- appointed executor who survived the testator.
#ASSERT `section 21(1) refuses a grant to` `Dinesh`
#ASSERT NOT `a grant may be made to` `Dinesh`
#ASSERT NOT `probate may be granted to` `Dinesh`
-- and he does not appear among those who may take the grant, though his
-- co-executor does.
#ASSERT count (`those who may take the grant in` `the estate with an infant executor`) EQUALS 1

§§§ `Security — s 29`

-- s 29(7) reaches "administrations whether with or without the will annexed",
-- so an executor taking probate gives no security.
#ASSERT NOT `security for the due administration of the estate is required in` `the Alice probate`
-- An administrator on intestacy, granted by the General Division, does.
#ASSERT `security for the due administration of the estate is required in` `the Bernard intestacy`
-- s 29(2): the bond is by the grantee and 2 sureties.
#ASSERT `the number of sureties required in` `the Bernard intestacy` EQUALS 2
#ASSERT `the amount of the bond in` `the Bernard intestacy` EQUALS 400000
-- s 29(2) again, on the facts that decide the recorded ambiguity. The estate
-- is sworn at $400,000 and $150,000 of the deceased's debts are secured by
-- mortgage. On reading (i), which is what is encoded -- "without deduction of
-- any debts due by the deceased, OTHER THAN debts secured by mortgage" carves
-- the mortgage out of the prohibition on deducting -- the bond is $250,000.
-- On reading (ii) it would be the full $400,000. Every other fixture has no
-- mortgage, so this is the only assertion that tells the readings apart.
#ASSERT `security for the due administration of the estate is required in` `the Bernard intestacy with a mortgaged flat`
#ASSERT `the number of sureties required in` `the Bernard intestacy with a mortgaged flat` EQUALS 2
#ASSERT `the amount of the bond in` `the Bernard intestacy with a mortgaged flat` EQUALS 250000
-- s 29(8): since 2014 a Family Court grantee gives no security unless the
-- beneficiary is an infant or the court thinks fit. Neither here.
#ASSERT NOT `security for the due administration of the estate is required in` `the Family Court administration`
#ASSERT `the number of sureties required in` `the Family Court administration` EQUALS 0

§§§ `The timeline — s 55 and WA 1838 s 28(2)`

-- s 55(1)(a): 6 months from the death of a deceased person. Alice and Bernard
-- both died on 15 January 2026, so the date is 15 July 2026.
#ASSERT `the date 6 months after the death in` `the Bernard intestacy` EQUALS YMD 2026 7 15

-- On 30 June 2026 the six months have not run: the family still has the
-- matter to itself.
#ASSERT NOT `section 55(1)(a) is engaged in` `the neglected estate as at 30 June 2026`
-- On 16 July 2026, with still no application, the Public Trustee -- or such
-- other person as the court thinks fit -- may be granted letters of
-- administration.
#ASSERT `section 55(1)(a) is engaged in` `the neglected estate as at 16 July 2026`
#ASSERT `letters of administration may be granted to the Public Trustee under section 55 in` `the neglected estate as at 16 July 2026`

-- s 55(2): the six months are not a waiting period for the Public Trustee.
#ASSERT `the Public Trustee may apply before the 6 months expire in` `the neglected estate as at 30 June 2026`

-- s 55(1)(c): one month from the date of the grant to give the security. The
-- grant issued on 10 March 2026, so the date is 10 April 2026.
#ASSERT `the date one month after the grant in` `the Alice probate` EQUALS JUST (YMD 2026 4 10)
-- It does not bite here, because probate requires no security under s 29(7).
#ASSERT NOT `section 55(1)(c) is engaged in` `the Alice probate`

-- s 55(1)(f): 6 months after the death of the last surviving administrator,
-- who took his grant on 10 February 2026 and died on 28 February 2026.
#ASSERT `the date 6 months after the death of the last surviving executor or administrator in` `the stranded estate` EQUALS JUST (YMD 2026 8 28)
#ASSERT `section 55(1)(f) is engaged in` `the stranded estate`
-- The facts that engage the limb are facts in which an original application
-- WAS made and a grant WAS taken out -- an administrator cannot die in office
-- otherwise -- so the limb must not be reading the original application. It
-- reads the further application under s 25(2), and closes when that is made.
#ASSERT `the stranded estate`'s `an application has been made for probate or letters of administration`
#ASSERT NOT `section 55(1)(f) is engaged in` `the estate whose administrator died and whose family applied again`
#ASSERT NOT `letters of administration may be granted to the Public Trustee under section 55 in` `the estate whose administrator died and whose family applied again`

-- WA 1838 s 28(2): an application to rectify the will must be made no later
-- than 6 months after the date on which a grant is first made -- 10 September
-- 2026 -- except with the permission of a court.
#ASSERT `the last date for an application to rectify the will in` `the Alice probate` EQUALS JUST (YMD 2026 9 10)
-- No grant, no clock. (This fixture is now overdetermined -- it is also
-- intestate -- so the two assertions below carry the discriminating facts.)
#ASSERT `the last date for an application to rectify the will in` `the Bernard intestacy` EQUALS NOTHING

-- WA 1838 s 28(1): the order is that "A WILL be rectified", so on an
-- intestacy there is nothing to rectify and no deadline -- however the grant
-- is dated. The Family Court administration is Bernard's intestacy with a
-- grant made on 10 March 2026, which is the case the previous assertion
-- cannot reach: it has no grant date, so it would pass whether or not the
-- deadline were confined to testate estates.
#ASSERT `the deceased died intestate in` `the Family Court administration`
#ASSERT `the last date for an application to rectify the will in` `the Family Court administration` EQUALS NOTHING

-- WA 1838 s 28(4)(b): a grant "that does not permit the distribution of the
-- testator's estate" is disregarded in fixing when a grant is first made.
-- PAA s 20 limits the grant pending a probate action in exactly those terms,
-- so the same will and the same 10 March 2026 grant date that give a
-- 10 September deadline on the probate give no deadline here: the clock has
-- not started.
#ASSERT `the name of the grant in` `the contested Alice estate after a limited grant` EQUALS "letters of administration pending probate action"
#ASSERT `Wills Act section 28(4) requires the grant to be disregarded in` `the contested Alice estate after a limited grant`
#ASSERT `the last date for an application to rectify the will in` `the contested Alice estate after a limited grant` EQUALS NOTHING

-- The timeline an app draws for the Alice probate: the death, the s 55(1)(a)
-- date, the grant, the s 55(1)(c) date, and the WA s 28(2) date.
#ASSERT count (`the timeline in` `the Alice probate`) EQUALS 5
-- Four for an intestate estate with a grant: there is no will to rectify, so
-- the WA s 28(2) event is not drawn.
#ASSERT count (`the timeline in` `the Family Court administration`) EQUALS 4
-- Four again where the only grant is the s 20 grant WA s 28(4)(b) disregards,
-- even though there is a will.
#ASSERT count (`the timeline in` `the contested Alice estate after a limited grant`) EQUALS 4
-- Before any grant, only the two events measured from the death.
#ASSERT count (`the timeline in` `the neglected estate as at 30 June 2026`) EQUALS 2

-- s 37: between the death and the grant, an intestate's estate is vested in
-- the Public Trustee -- not in the family.
#ASSERT `the estate vests in the Public Trustee under section 37 in` `the neglected estate as at 30 June 2026`
#ASSERT NOT `the estate vests in the Public Trustee under section 37 in` `the Alice probate`

§§§ `What comes off the top — ss 57, 58, 66, 67 and the Schedules`

-- s 57(4): a solvent estate is applied in the order of the Second Schedule.
#ASSERT `the schedule that applies in` `the Alice probate` EQUALS "Second Schedule"
-- s 57(1): an insolvent one is administered under the First Schedule, which
-- gives the funeral, testamentary and administration expenses priority and
-- then hands the rest to the law of bankruptcy.
#ASSERT `the schedule that applies in` `the Bernard intestacy, insolvent` EQUALS "First Schedule"

-- The Second Schedule has 8 numbered items and the First Schedule 2, and the
-- app shows them in that order.
#ASSERT count `the Second Schedule` EQUALS 8
#ASSERT count `the First Schedule` EQUALS 2
#ASSERT count (`the order of application of assets in` `the Alice probate`) EQUALS 8

-- Second Schedule paragraph 8(a): the order may be varied by the will. This
-- will does not vary it.
#ASSERT `the statutory order governs in` `the Alice probate`

-- s 58(1): the mortgage goes with the flat.
#ASSERT `the interest charged is primarily liable in` `the mortgaged flat`
-- s 58(3)(a): a general direction to pay debts out of the residuary estate is
-- NOT a contrary intention, so the flat still carries its own mortgage.
#ASSERT `the interest charged is primarily liable in` `the mortgaged flat with a general direction to pay debts`
-- Words expressly referring to the charge do shift it.
#ASSERT NOT `the interest charged is primarily liable in` `the mortgaged flat with an express direction about the mortgage`
-- s 58(2): a half-share of a $500,000 flat bears half of a $300,000 charge.
#ASSERT `the proportionate part of the charge borne by` `the mortgaged flat` 250000 300000 EQUALS 150000

-- s 66(1): a commission "not exceeding 5% on the value of the assets
-- collected by them" -- $200,000 collected, so at most $10,000, and that only
-- in the court's discretion.
#ASSERT `the maximum commission under section 66 in` `the Alice probate` EQUALS 10000

-- s 67(1): three heads of expense the court SHALL allow.
#ASSERT count `the heads of expense the court shall allow under section 67` EQUALS 3

§§§ `How long it takes — s 69`

-- s 69: an uncontested matter is dealt with by the registrar, exercising the
-- powers of the court.
#ASSERT `the registrar may exercise the court's powers in` `the Alice probate`
#ASSERT NOT `the registrar may exercise the court's powers in` `the contested Alice estate`
